roboclient - a small IPv4 DHCP client for OPENSTEP

Files installed:
  /usr/local/sbin/roboclient                  the client
  /usr/local/share/roboclient/roboclient.script   applies a lease (ifconfig, route)
  /usr/local/etc/roboclient.conf              settings (copied from roboclient.conf.dist
                                              on first install, never overwritten)
  /usr/local/etc/rc.dhcp                      boot-time wrapper, described below
  /usr/local/var/roboclient/<if>.info         the current lease

Using it by hand:  roboclient -m 0:0:c0:12:34:56 en0   (see roboclient(8))


Configuring the network at boot with DHCP instead of BOOTP
----------------------------------------------------------

rc.dhcp gets an address on one interface with roboclient, waits until it has a lease,
prints what happened, and leaves roboclient running in the background to renew the
lease.  If no DHCP server answers it prints an error and returns 1; /etc/rc then simply
carries on, without an address on that interface.  (pkg does not edit /etc/rc.)

1. Set the interface's hardware address in /usr/local/etc/roboclient.conf.  At boot the
   interface has no address to look it up from, so this is required:

       MAC_en0=0:0:c0:12:34:56

   The other settings are optional (see roboclient.conf):

       RC_DHCP_INTERFACE=en0       interface to configure (default en0)
       RC_DHCP_TRIES=3             DISCOVER attempts before giving up (default 3, about
                                   28 seconds; 0 waits forever)
       RC_DHCP_OPTS="-h myhost"    extra options for roboclient
       RC_DHCP_STATUS_CMD=...      a command called with each status line

2. In /etc/rc, where the network interface is configured with BOOTP, call

       /usr/local/etc/rc.dhcp

   in its place.  /usr/local must be mounted by then.  Keep a copy of /etc/rc first.

What it prints (this goes to the console, so it shows on the boot screen):

       DHCP: requesting an address on en0...
       DHCP: en0 is 192.168.1.50 netmask 255.255.255.0, router 192.168.1.1, dns 192.168.1.1, lease 86400s

   or, with no answer:

       DHCP: ERROR - en0 is not configured: no DHCP offers received on en0
       DHCP: continuing without a network address on en0 (see /usr/local/var/roboclient/rc.dhcp.log)

   Other errors it reports the same way: roboclient not installed, no MAC_<interface>
   set, and "roboclient is already running" when rc.dhcp is run twice.

Test it without rebooting, on a machine whose network you can afford to reconfigure:

       sh /usr/local/etc/rc.dhcp; echo $?
